        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Label, Line by Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with protein. The Amazon listing states 22 g of clear whey protein and 4.5 g of BCAAs per scoop, and a supplement database entry for the tub lists a 28 g scoop with 22 g of protein and 10 mg of sodium. That is a protein density of close to 80 percent by weight, which is what you would expect from a well-filtered isolate with very little filler.
          </p>

          <p>
            Calories are less clearly published. Nutrition databases list a SEEQ scoop at about 100 calories, and independent reviews repeat the same number. With 22 g of protein contributing roughly 88 of those calories, the rest is a trace of carbohydrate from flavoring and acids. Either way, this is one of the leanest ways to buy protein, and it suits people counting calories closely.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ingredient list on the stick pack listing reads: whey protein isolate, natural flavors, sucralose, citric acid, malic acid, polysorbate 80 and/or sunflower lecithin, spirulina powder for color, Reb A stevia and silicone as an anti-foaming agent. The acids give the drink its tart, juice-like edge, and the anti-foaming agent is there because clear whey is notorious for foaming when shaken.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergen notes are reasonable. SEEQ says its tubs are made in a facility free of nuts and soy, and the products are gluten-free. It is still a dairy product, so anyone with a true milk allergy should not drink it, even though the lactose figure is listed as 0 g. People with lactose intolerance often tolerate isolate well, but tolerance varies, and a doctor or dietitian is the right person to ask if you have reacted badly to dairy before.
          </p>

