        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sweeteners and the Formula Question
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the part of SEEQ that deserves the most care. The stick pack listing on Amazon names sucralose and Reb A stevia as sweeteners. A supplement database entry for a SEEQ tub instead lists monk fruit and Reb M, a stevia-derived sweetener, with no sucralose. Earlier independent reviews of SEEQ also flagged sucralose as a con. And at least one warehouse club listing describes the product as having no artificial sweeteners.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most likely explanation is that the formula has changed over time or differs by flavor and format. That is common with fast-growing brands, which tweak recipes as they move between co-manufacturers and retail partners. It is not a scandal, but it does mean you cannot assume the tub on your doorstep matches a review you read last year, or even the listing you clicked on.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you avoid sucralose, read the ingredient panel on the physical product before you drink it, and check the specific flavor. If you are fine with sucralose, the issue mostly disappears, since sucralose and stevia are both widely used and approved sweeteners at the amounts found in protein powder. Some people do report stomach upset from stevia or sugar alcohols, so if you have had trouble before, start with a single stick pack.
          </p>

          <p>
            The color question is simpler. SEEQ uses spirulina powder for color on the listing we checked, rather than synthetic dyes, which will appeal to buyers who read labels for artificial colors. Polysorbate 80 and silicone are processing aids that improve clarity and reduce foam. They are permitted food additives, and the amounts are small, but they do mean SEEQ is not a short, kitchen-ingredient label.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT TO DO</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Read your tub, not the review.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Sweeteners appear to vary between SEEQ listings. If sucralose or stevia is a dealbreaker, check the ingredient panel of the exact flavor you buy before you open it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clear whey is expensive across the board because isolate costs more to produce than concentrate and the scoops are smaller. SEEQ sits at the upper end even within that group. At the prices we saw on Amazon and Target at the time of writing, a SEEQ tub works out at very roughly 2.20 to 2.80 dollars per 25 g of protein, depending on size, retailer and whether a sale is running.
          </p>

          <p>
            The direct 25-serving tub has historically been the best value of SEEQ's formats, while the smaller Target tub costs more per serving because you are paying for a shelf-friendly size. Stick packs cost the most per gram, as single-serve packaging always does. They make sense as a sampler or for travel, not as your everyday supply.
          </p>

          <p>
            For comparison, large tubs of mainstream whey concentrate usually land around 1 to 1.50 dollars per 25 g at typical prices, and drop below that on a good sale. So you are paying roughly double for the clear format. That premium buys a different drinking experience, not more or better protein. Our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> guide runs the cost maths if price is your main concern.
          </p>

