        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Testing and Certification
          </h2>

          <p>
            SEEQ does not display NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport, Informed Choice or Clean Label Project certification on the listings we checked, and we could not find it in those programs' public databases at the time of writing. Independent reviewers who have covered SEEQ list it as not third-party tested. A supplement database page described it as backed by third-party testing but noted that it could not verify product-specific results.
          </p>

          <p>
            That gap matters for two groups. Drug-tested athletes need a sport certification, because only those programs screen batches for banned substances and stand behind the result. And buyers who are worried about contaminants, such as pregnant women or parents buying for teenagers, usually want either a certificate or published test results they can read. SEEQ currently offers neither in a form we could confirm.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is worth keeping the risk in perspective. Whey isolate generally tests lower for heavy metals than many plant proteins, and nothing we found suggests a problem with SEEQ specifically. The point is not that SEEQ is unsafe. It is that you are taking the brand's word for its quality, rather than an independent lab's, and that is a reasonable thing to weigh when the price is this high.
          </p>

          <p>
            If certification is non-negotiable for you, Thorne's whey isolate carries NSF Certified for Sport status per the brand and NSF, and Isopure is a long-established isolate brand that is easy to find in stores. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> explainer covers what each certification checks and what it does not, so you can decide how much the badge is worth to you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Taste, Mixing and Foam
          </h2>

          <p>
            We have not tasted SEEQ, so everything here is buyer feedback. Flavor is the main reason people buy it, and many reviews describe the fruit flavors as tasting closer to a sports drink or juice than a protein shake. The Amazon ratings are more mixed than the social media buzz suggests, though: the tub listing we checked sat at about 3.6 out of 5 stars and the stick pack listing at about 3.9.
          </p>

          <p>
            The complaints follow the usual clear whey pattern. Some buyers find the flavors too sweet or too sour, some notice a faint dairy note behind the fruit, and some dislike foam. SEEQ's own directions tell you to mix one scoop with 14 to 16 oz of water, shake or froth, then let it settle for about 30 seconds before adding ice. That settling step is not optional if you hate foam.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water volume makes a big difference. Clear whey tastes harsher and more sour when concentrated, so if a flavor seems aggressive, add more water before you give up on the tub. Very cold water helps too. Mixing with milk defeats the purpose, since the acids in clear whey can curdle dairy and the whole point of the format is a light, transparent drink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because flavor is subjective and the tubs are not cheap, the stick pack variety box is the sensible way to start. It includes three of the best-selling flavors, so you can find the one you like before committing to a full tub. If none of them work for you, our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">best clear protein powder</a> guide covers other clear wheys with different flavor ranges.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Buy It, and Who Should Skip
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy SEEQ if you struggle to drink milky shakes, especially in hot weather or after cardio, and a juice-like drink would get you to your protein target more reliably. A product you actually finish beats a cheaper one you leave in the cupboard. It also suits people who want a very lean protein, with about 100 calories and no sugar per scoop, for a calorie-controlled diet.
          </p>

          <p>
            SEEQ's listings also mention GLP-1 support. To be clear, the powder has no effect on GLP-1 medication itself. What it offers is an easy, low-volume way to get protein when appetite is low, which is a common challenge for people on those drugs. Anyone taking a GLP-1 medication should follow their prescriber's or dietitian's guidance on protein intake, since this is not medical advice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip SEEQ if you are a drug-tested athlete, because there is no sport certification to rely on. Skip it if you are on a tight budget, because standard whey delivers the same protein for roughly half the cost. Skip it if you avoid sucralose and cannot check the exact flavor's label. And skip it if you want a creamy shake, a baking powder or a plant protein, since SEEQ does not make any of those.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pregnant or breastfeeding readers, people with kidney disease and anyone on medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding any protein supplement, SEEQ included. That is standard advice rather than a concern about this product, but it carries extra weight when a brand has no independent testing you can point to.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. SEEQ vs Other Clear Wheys
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against Isopure Infusions, SEEQ offers a slightly bigger scoop, 22 g against about 20 g, and a more candy-like flavor list. Isopure has a much longer track record as an isolate brand and is easier to find in pharmacies and big-box stores. Neither is the cheapest protein you can buy. If you want more on Isopure's range, our <a href="/isopure-protein-powder-review/">Isopure protein powder review</a> covers the full lineup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against Myprotein Clear Whey Isolate, SEEQ usually costs more, while Myprotein runs frequent sales that can pull its price well below SEEQ's. Myprotein's serving is about 20 g of protein per the label. Our <a href="/myprotein-review/">Myprotein review</a> explains how its pricing and discount codes work, which is the main reason it wins on value for many buyers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against a regular whey isolate such as Thorne's, the comparison is about format. Thorne gives you about 21 g of protein with NSF Certified for Sport status, but it mixes into a conventional shake rather than a clear drink. If you mainly care about verified quality, Thorne wins. If you mainly care about drinking something that tastes like juice, SEEQ or another clear whey is the better fit.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other clear wheys reviewed on the site follow the same trade-off. Oath, Bloom, Ryse and Ghost all sell clear proteins at premium prices, and the differences come down to flavor list, scoop size and certification. Our <a href="/oath-protein-powder-review/">Oath protein powder review</a> covers another clear whey that grew through social media, which makes it the most direct comparison to SEEQ.
          </p>
        </div>

